      "defend": {
        "setId": "e2_recruitment_inbox",
        "stage": null,
        "reportAlwaysVisible": true
      },
      "strings": {
        "stepAway": "Look up from the kiosk and carry on in the room for a minute. Someone may ask you something. Your place is saved.",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional company. First you're on the red team: look at what Tarrowby has posted about its open jobs, then write one job application to its recruiter, Fatima Begum, with an attachment picked from a list of labels. Nothing real is attached or sent. Then you swap seats and sort a recruitment inbox like the one your email landed in. Only the inbox is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the application you just sent, the way it looked to Fatima, Tarrowby's recruitment lead. The parts picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Fatima opened it between two interviews, forty applications still unread. One more CV for the warehouse job, from someone who named the role, so she opened the attachment. Forty applications deep, anyone opens the next one.",
        "flipDefenderLineByChoice": {
          "pretext_application": "Fatima opened it between two interviews with forty applications still unread. It named the warehouse vacancy she was recruiting for and carried a CV, so it read as one more real applicant and she opened the attachment.",
          "pretext_general": "A speculative 'in case anything comes up' note names no role, so it has less pull, yet a quiet CV from a stranger still gets opened on a busy day. Whatever the covering line, the attachment is the part that does the work."
        },
        "flipNoSend": "Your application didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of message arrives in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "flipMissed": "The defence is the same either way: next, the same kind of message lands in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It opened like any real application, with nothing off to pick up on. Next, the same kind of message lands in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "noBlameLine": "Every one of these was built to look right, and opening attachments from strangers is a recruiter's whole job. A miss here points to a busy inbox, and to where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: read CVs in the viewer. A real CV never needs macros or Enable Content, so if one asks for that, or for any second step before it will show, don't take it: ask for a plain copy and report it. If a CV 'doesn't come through' and sits behind a link that wants you to sign in, ask the candidate to send it as an attachment instead. Then report anything odd using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These were built by people who know exactly what a real application looks like, and opening a CV is a recruiter's job, not a lapse. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "Why a CV that wants a second step matters",
            "text": "Office documents can carry macros, small programs that run inside the file. Since mid-2022, Office blocks macros in files that come from the internet or email by default, so a booby-trapped CV on its own is stopped behind a red security bar. Attackers now need you to take one more step for them: switch content on, unpack a zip with a password from the email, or follow a 'view my CV' link and sign in. A real CV just opens, in the viewer or in Protected View, Word's read-only mode for files from email, and never needs any of that.\n\nTo a real attacker, that second step is a foothold on an HR machine full of candidate records, and from there a way into the candidate database and the recruitment mailbox. The habit you just practised, giving any CV that wants a second step a second look, is what breaks that chain, because the step is never taken and the file never runs.\n\nWhen a file like this does get its second step, it runs on the HR machine, which goes out of action while it is rebuilt, and HR spend the afternoon re-contacting candidates by hand. Because CVs are personal data, under UK GDPR the organisation assesses the breach without undue delay and, where it has to report, tells the ICO within 72 hours; candidates are told where the risk to them is high."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Reporting it",
            "text": "The attacker picked the driver vacancy, looked up the fleet coordinator, and sent the CV straight to Chidi, past the recruitment inbox. Office blocked it, so it needed Chidi to follow the steps in the email and switch that protection off himself. He reported it instead, IT blocked the sender, and the file never ran.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca02_cv_choice === 'report'"
          },
          {
            "heading": "What one more step let in",
            "text": "Chidi followed the candidate's steps and switched Office's protection off himself. That ran the attacker's code. They wanted a hiring manager's machine, because applicants' details sit on it. In the seconds before IT's alert cut it off, they copied the driver applications: names, addresses, licence details. Tarrowby is now working out whether it has to report to the ICO and who to tell. The gap was one step: Office had blocked the file, and the email talked him round the block.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca02_cv_choice === 'enable'"
          },
          {
            "heading": "What one more step let in",
            "text": "Chidi asked, got no answer and went ahead: he followed the candidate's steps and switched Office's protection off himself. That ran the attacker's code. They wanted a hiring manager's machine, because applicants' details sit on it. In the seconds before IT's alert cut it off, they copied the driver applications: names, addresses, licence details. Tarrowby is now working out whether it has to report to the ICO and who to tell. The gap was one step: Office had blocked the file, and the email talked him round the block.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca02_cv_choice === 'silent'"
          }
        ]
